{"id": "d51bbe22-1167-47ec-8a6c-cff11b5ec9fe", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review your EBS setup in us-east-1 against production-readiness criteria. That covers encryption, snapshot and backup coverage, volume types and performance, and unattached or at-risk volumes. It means scanning volumes, snapshots, and metrics, so I'm running the review in the background and will summarize what I find.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_OArhJiwpqSAPeid9PaoNAe", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-readiness-review", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics", "discovering-topology"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Review the EBS storage setup in us-east-1 for production-readiness ahead of a launch next week. Produce a concise, prioritized assessment (read-only; do NOT make any changes).",
  "context": "User asks 'Is my storage production-ready?'. This is a shallow first pass. Start with account 012345678901_1 (the agent space's account), and also check any other AWS accounts associated with the agent space (use list_associations) that are monitor/source accounts, region us-east-1 only. Current time is 2026-11-23T19:44:49Z.",
  "resources": ["EBS volumes, snapshots, and EC2 attachments in us-east-1", "AWS Backup plans/Data Lifecycle Manager policies covering EBS", "CloudWatch EBS metrics (AWS/EBS namespace)"],
  "timeframe": "Metrics: last 14 days (2026-11-09T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-23T19:44:49Z)",
  "criteria": "Check and report on: (1) Encryption: unencrypted volumes/snapshots, whether account-level default EBS encryption is enabled in us-east-1, KMS key used (AWS-managed vs CMK). (2) Backup/DR: which in-use volumes have recent snapshots (age of newest), coverage by DLM or AWS Backup, any volumes with no protection, snapshot age/staleness, fast snapshot restore, snapshot block public access state. (3) Volume types/config: gp2 volumes that could be gp3, io1/io2 usage, provisioned IOPS/throughput vs. observed usage, Multi-Attach usage, DeleteOnTermination flags on non-root data volumes of important instances. (4) Performance/health: from CloudWatch, volumes with high queue length, burst balance depletion (gp2/st1/sc1), throughput/IOPS near provisioned limits, VolumeStatus/impaired checks, any volumes in error or impaired state, EC2 instance attachments to EBS-optimized instance types. (5) Hygiene/cost: unattached (available) volumes, orphaned snapshots, volumes attached to stopped instances. (6) Tagging: volumes missing tags such as Name/Environment/Owner. Give counts, specific volume/snapshot IDs for any issues, severity (blocker / should-fix / nice-to-have), and an overall readiness verdict. Keep the report compact; list only exceptions by ID rather than every volume. Suggest areas that could be examined deeper."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS volumes, snapshots, and metrics in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

